WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.620
Hey, Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:08.580
Once upon a time, in a land far, far away (well,&nbsp;
Africa), there lived a group of odd-looking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:13.800
standy-uppy creatures called homo sapiens.&nbsp;
Early humans had been living in Africa for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.120
hundreds of thousands of years, chasing&nbsp;
gazelles and running away from lions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:25.560
but homo sapiens was a little bit different.&nbsp;
If Homo neanderthalensis was stocky and Homo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.800
Erectus was cocky, then Homo sapiens was&nbsp;
simply the savviest simian on the savannah.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:38.460
Technologically innovative, highly adaptable,&nbsp;
and capable of organising into complex social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:44.100
structures, when homo sapiens decided to&nbsp;
up and leave Africa about 70,000 years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:50.160
the species was taking steps that would change&nbsp;
the world forever. In just a few decamillenia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.380
these dauntless drifters discovered every nook&nbsp;
of the earth, from Siberia's shivering tundras&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:02.280
to Australia's arid expanses; from America's&nbsp;
grand grasslands to the verdant valleys of Asia.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.120
During that time they - or should I say&nbsp;
we - also learned to fly, built cities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:16.620
explored the solar system, harnessed the power&nbsp;
of the atom, and invented the sandwich. But all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:22.680
that progress has come at a price - today, we’re&nbsp;
arguably in danger of outgrowing our home planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.380
which has us wondering whether it's&nbsp;
time for the next grand human migration.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.700
If you think messing up a planet and then&nbsp;
buggering off is a bit like farting in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:18.000
lift and running away, then you’d be right, but&nbsp;
let’s not get lost in ethical debates. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.640
we need to figure out where the hell we’re going.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:27.540
The smart money says Mars. And I say ‘smart&nbsp;
money’ because Elon Musk is both smart and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.820
has lots of money, and Mars seems to be&nbsp;
where he’s off to. But is he right? Could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:37.380
human beings really travel to, and&nbsp;
eventually colonise, the Red Planet?

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:43.380
Mars is a particularly appealing option for human&nbsp;
migration for several reasons. For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:47.640
it's one of the closest planets to Earth at an&nbsp;
average distance of about 140 million miles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:52.440
It alvso has similar length days to&nbsp;
Earth, so even though it only receives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.460
about 44% of the sunlight, at least you&nbsp;
won’t need to change your alarm clock.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:02.280
Mars is also rich in useful elements like&nbsp;
magnesium, silicon, and of course, iron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:08.520
which puts the ‘red’ in Red Planet. Important&nbsp;
resources like these could all be mined when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:15.060
get there, reducing the amount of expensive excess&nbsp;
luggage we’ll need to book on SpaceX’s Starship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:21.840
Finally, and most importantly, Mars lies&nbsp;
on the cusp of our solar system’s habitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.960
zone - the area around the sun in which&nbsp;
liquid water could exist on an orbiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:32.820
planet’s surface. Water once flowed&nbsp;
freely on the Red Planet, and we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:40.260
that plenty quality H20 can still be found in&nbsp;
its ice caps. If we found a way to melt them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:46.920
we could create a life-giving ocean between 5&nbsp;
and 11 metres deep across most of the planet.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:53.640
Unfortunately, the good news ends&nbsp;
there, and the bad news is… well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:59.460
really fucking bad. With surface air pressure&nbsp;
about 1% of our own, Mars has all the atmosphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:06.060
of a kebab shop on a Tuesday morning. Liquid water&nbsp;
would boil away in seconds, just like your blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.860
if you dared to step outside without&nbsp;
protection. So… yeah. Don’t do that.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:15.240
The measly Martian atmosphere isn’t just thin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:20.640
it’s also 95% carbon dioxide, which&nbsp;
is deadly to humans in high doses and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.360
ironically, a major reason we might&nbsp;
be heading to Mars in the first place.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:31.020
Earth’s thick atmosphere and strong magnetic&nbsp;
field shield us from dangerous cosmic and solar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:38.880
radiation. Mars has no magnetic field, and without&nbsp;
those twin shields the Martian surface is a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:45.300
dangerous place indeed, with levels of ionising&nbsp;
radiation 40-50 times higher than on Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:50.100
That’s enough to dramatically increase&nbsp;
the risk of cancer and can potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.920
cause acute radiation sickness or even death.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:58.380
Mars is also home to some pretty extreme&nbsp;
weather. Dust storms, with winds hitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:06.240
250 mph, can last for days or weeks, and&nbsp;
can occasionally engulf the entire planet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:12.180
They would pose a serious threat to equipment,&nbsp;
space suits, and our makeshift Martian shelters.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:19.440
But wait, there’s more! Martian gravity is only&nbsp;
a third of Earth's, and don’t know exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.640
long-term effect that would have on the&nbsp;
human body, but astronauts who’ve spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:28.200
significant time in space have suffered from&nbsp;
muscle atrophy and a loss of bone density.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:34.740
Even in a protective suit, living on Mars might&nbsp;
fast-track you to having a 90-year-old's body.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:45.120
And Mars is cold – I mean, really cold. Think&nbsp;
-62°C on average and -140°C on a nippy winter day.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:52.200
Just in case you’re the hangry type, I should&nbsp;
probably also mention that Martian soil is toxic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:57.900
so we won’t be able to safely&nbsp;
grow any food. Not even Mars bars.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:03.960
Lastly, let's talk about social logistics.&nbsp;
Colonizing Mars would be like an eternal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:12.300
Christmas with the in-laws. Confined spaces and&nbsp;
clashing personalities can lead to depression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.240
anxiety, and a range of homicidal thoughts.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:24.660
OK, so the basic summary here is that Mars hates&nbsp;
us and wants us all dead. The question is, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:29.640
can we do about it? Well, if there’s one thing I&nbsp;
learned during my previous career as the dictator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:35.820
of a small insular state, it’s that there’s&nbsp;
nothing nuclear weapons can’t fix. Oddly enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:41.880
Elon musk seems to agree, because he wants to make&nbsp;
Mars more habitable by bombarding it with 10,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:49.140
nukes. But there is some method to the madness of&nbsp;
declaring nuclear war on an uninhabited planet.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:55.920
The dream scenario when it comes to migrating&nbsp;
to Mars would be to terraform it - that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.440
using tech to make it habitable. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:02.220
we’d need to warm things up a bit,&nbsp;
hence World War III: Mars Edition.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:08.940
Musk plans to aim his atomic arsenal&nbsp;
at Mars’ polar ice caps, vaporising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:13.800
the large quantities of water and carbon dioxide&nbsp;
trapped within. Releasing these greenhouse gases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:19.080
could theoretically create a runaway greenhouse&nbsp;
effect, warming Mars to Earth-like temperatures.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:25.080
Unfortunately, this bold plan has some big&nbsp;
issues. For one thing, Bond villains aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:32.520
it's actually quite tricky for private individuals&nbsp;
to acquire nukes (sorry Elon). More importantly, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:38.880
2018 study concluded that nuking Mars, while kinda&nbsp;
cool, could trigger a nuclear winter, with dust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:44.760
and debris blocking out what meagre sunlight the&nbsp;
planet currently receives, making it even colder.

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:49.800
Another idea would be to melt&nbsp;
Mars’ ice caps not with bombs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:56.220
but with a mirror. The idea would be to stick&nbsp;
a giant aluminium mirror in orbit around Mars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.220 --> 00:09:01.080
concentrating sunlight on the southern&nbsp;
ice cap. It’s an innovative approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:05.460
but it too has drawbacks - to focus&nbsp;
enough energy to melt all that ice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:11.100
the mirror would need to be 250 kilometres&nbsp;
across - that’s about 20% bigger than Denmark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:18.180
Obviously, we wouldn’t be able to launch aluminium&nbsp;
Denmark into space from earth, so we’d have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:25.080
build this monstrous mirror in orbit. Considering&nbsp;
the International Space Station, at a staggering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:31.860
94 metres across, is our largest manmade space&nbsp;
object, it's clear we're not quite there yet.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:42.300
So, directly melting Mars’ ice caps seems tough -&nbsp;
but maybe we could do it indirectly - by borrowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:48.180
some lessons from our own planet's past. In the&nbsp;
late 1980s, chlorofluorocarbons, better known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:54.480
as CFCs, were banned worldwide because they were&nbsp;
destroying the ozone layer. But it turns out CFCs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.480 --> 00:10:00.660
are also incredibly effective greenhouse gases&nbsp;
- 10,000 times as potent as CO2. Here on Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:07.860
we’re terrified of them, but up on Mars CFCs&nbsp;
might be our new BFFs. We may even find the raw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:13.800
materials to produce them in Martian rocks, and&nbsp;
if we don’t there’s always the Elon Musk-friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:19.740
option of crashing thousands of spaceships loaded&nbsp;
with compressed CFCs into Mars. Either way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:24.120
pack Mars’ atmosphere with enough CFCs, and&nbsp;
we’d trigger a runaway greenhouse effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:29.460
sufficiently warming the planet to release&nbsp;
the CO2 stored in the ice caps, warming it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:33.780
either further, while simultaneously&nbsp;
thickening Mars’ weedy atmosphere.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:40.680
OK, so Mars is now warm enough for flips flops&nbsp;
and shorts and the atmosphere is thick enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:46.320
that your blood won’t boil every time you&nbsp;
pop to the corner shop for a newspaper. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:51.600
beefed-up atmosphere means extra protection from&nbsp;
solar wind and cosmic radiation, which we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:58.140
boost even further by generating an artificial&nbsp;
magnetosphere around the entire planet. Scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:02.280
have come up with several ways we might do that&nbsp;
- including building a giant superconducting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:09.000
ring around Mars’ equator or installing a&nbsp;
magnetic shield between the Sun and Mars.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:14.460
Great though the new Martian atmosphere&nbsp;
would be, we shouldn’t forget the minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:20.400
problem that it would be almost entirely lacking&nbsp;
in oxygen and therefore, totally unbreathable.

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:27.180
NASA’s perseverance rover has already successfully&nbsp;
converted Martian CO2 into Oxygen right there on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:32.040
the surface using a device called MOXIE,&nbsp;
but scaling that up to an entire planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:40.260
might not be possible. Another option would&nbsp;
be unleashing oxygen-producing microorganisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:46.380
to slowly increase O2 levels. It might sound&nbsp;
far-fetched, but that’s exactly what happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:52.860
right here on earth about 2.5 billion years&nbsp;
ago, when cyanobacteria turned our once methane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:58.200
and ammonia-rich atmosphere into the tasty&nbsp;
oxygen-rich version that keeps us alive today.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:04.380
As surface conditions slowly improve, we&nbsp;
can introduce a few hardy plant species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:10.320
to convert even more CO2 into O2. First, we’ll&nbsp;
need to detox the soil of nasty toxins, but NASA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:16.320
has already grown healthy plants in simulated&nbsp;
Martian soil, so it’s theoretically possible.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:22.020
But merely upping the oxygen levels wouldn’t&nbsp;
be enough. Earth’s atmosphere is 78% nitrogen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:27.660
an element that is inconveniently scarce&nbsp;
on Mars. So, to make Mars breathable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:33.180
we’d have to import nitrogen on a massive scale.&nbsp;
One way to do that would be to round up numerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:39.900
nitrogen-rich asteroids and just… crash them&nbsp;
into Mars. A more grown-up approach would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:47.820
venturing to Saturn’s largest moon, Titan, which&nbsp;
boasts a 95% nitrogen-rich atmosphere. Admittedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:53.220
shipping several trillion tons of liquified&nbsp;
nitrogen from Titan to Mars wouldn’t exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:57.540
be a cakewalk - but if we’re honest, that’s&nbsp;
pretty much been the theme of this video.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:04.200
Terraforming Mars is certainly possible, it’s&nbsp;
just going to be really, really difficult. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:09.720
difficult that NASA scientist Chris McKay&nbsp;
thinks it'll take a cool 100,000 years.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:16.500
But we don’t necessarily have to terraform Mars to&nbsp;
live there - we could go for a slightly ambitious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:23.100
approach and simply build artificial habitats on&nbsp;
- or possibly even beneath - the Martian surface.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:28.860
Sure, it's a tall order, but it seems&nbsp;
solvable in the coming decades. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:33.600
Elon Musk - yeah him again -&nbsp;
already has plans to build a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:38.760
fully functioning ‘Mars city’ with&nbsp;
up to a million inhabitants by 2050.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:45.420
That guy really needs a hobby. The minor drawback&nbsp;
to this particular mad musk plan is the cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:52.920
at roughly 10 trillion dollars - that’s around 10%&nbsp;
of the GDP of every country on earth put together.

00:13:55.080 --> 00:14:01.800
So, could humans really migrate to Mars? The&nbsp;
answer is… absolutely. As for whether or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:07.560
we actually will before we blow ourselves&nbsp;
up, die from a pandemic, get vaporised by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:12.840
a giant asteroid or supervolcano, lose the&nbsp;
war with the robots, get enslaved by aliens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:19.080
or accidentally starve to death while hanging out&nbsp;
in a VR world (where people have fucking legs)…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:22.140
your guess is as good as mine.

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:23.880
Thanks for watching.

